# UPSTREAM TARBALLS ARE IN THE TREE, THROUGH LFS. A clone must be able to build
# the ISO with no second fetch: the `sha256 =` in each recipe is what verifies
# an archive, and a hash with nothing to hash is a promise nobody can check.
#
# THE FILTER MUST BE INSTALLED BEFORE THEY ARE ADDED. A tarball staged before
# `git lfs install` is an ordinary blob and stays one until the history is
# rewritten — and seven of these are over the 100 MB github.com refuses on a
# push, so the mistake does not surface until the push fails.
#
#     git lfs install --local
#     git lfs ls-files | wc -l        # must equal the count of files on disk
#
# THE QUOTA IS THE CONSTRAINT AND IT IS SHARED ACROSS THE WHOLE ACCOUNT. These
# are 7.1 GB against a free allowance of 10 GiB of storage and 10 GiB a month
# of bandwidth, counted over every repository the account owns. Exceeding it
# does not slow a clone down, it blocks LFS reads — including the 7 MB of
# vendored art and fixtures that have nothing to do with ports, so a fresh
# clone cannot check out at all. A paid data pack is what keeps this working;
# without one, the margin is under 3 GiB and one month's bandwidth is a handful
# of clones.
ports/core/**/*.tar.* filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
ports/core/**/*.tgz   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
ports/core/**/*.zip   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
